WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.083
The ablation study peels the method apart to show
that both pieces earn their keep.

00:00:04.163 --> 00:00:08.246
First, removing the bidirectional curriculum causes
a consistent drop in performance,

00:00:08.326 --> 00:00:12.409
demonstrating the value of gradually guiding the
agent from easy initial states and goals to harder ones.

00:00:12.489 --> 00:00:16.572
Then, further removing the auxiliary agent,

00:00:16.652 --> 00:00:20.735
which reduces the method to naive reset-free
reinforcement learning,

00:00:20.815 --> 00:00:24.898
causes an additional and larger drop, particularly
in the object-manipulation tasks.

00:00:24.978 --> 00:00:29.062
The gains are task-dependent: the bidirectional
curriculum matters little in the simple

00:00:29.142 --> 00:00:33.225
Tabletop Manipulation environment,

00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:37.388
and the auxiliary agent is less helpful in Point-U-Maze,

00:00:37.468 --> 00:00:41.551
where the start is already far from the goal.

00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:45.714
The authors also confirm the implicit curriculum
behaves as intended: the fraction of

00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:49.877
episodes the auxiliary agent intervenes in falls
toward zero once the forward agent is

00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:54.040
fully trained.
